WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.260
Little Ninja Brothers, now go out on your journey to save the once merry world, China Land.

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:29.840
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:31.700
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:37.040
And I'm Joe. So, China Land is a world, not a theme park?

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:42.080
Yeah, it's like, you know, there's like Somalia and Somaliland.

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.840
I didn't know that, actually.

00:00:44.460 --> 00:00:45.960
I honestly don't know the difference.

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:47.380
I don't know anything about it.

00:00:47.520 --> 00:00:50.140
I just know that's like, there's, you could say two things.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.660
Maybe it's referring to the same thing.

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:54.720
Okay. There's also Hollywood Land.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:56.320
Yes, yeah. No, okay.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:01:00.580
But what I'm saying is like, they're saying it's a whole world, the once merry world of

00:01:00.580 --> 00:01:03.880
China Land. So it's a land, but it's also a world.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.160
We all live in our own little world.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:09.340
I guess China Land world is flat.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:11.840
Flat, baby. Well, yeah.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:13.400
I talk about flat Earth so much.

00:01:13.680 --> 00:01:15.300
Well, yeah. I talk about the flat Earth so much.

00:01:15.400 --> 00:01:17.480
I'm not sure how often we talk about it on the show.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.620
Like- We don't. Is this like our fourth flat world joke?

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:26.580
The Earth is round for anybody who wants to hear it.

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:28.320
Spherical, one might say.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:29.720
If anybody wants to hear it.

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.480
Yeah, exactly. The Earth is round.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:38.780
And this game is a top-down, unfortunately it looks flat, overworld.

00:01:39.080 --> 00:01:42.200
Unfortunately. That's a negative in this, for the game.

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:46.440
No, it's just a negative for me because I was saying that the world is round and then

00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:47.700
here we are. Okay.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:51.740
I'm sure that you could get to the end of the world, at least in Kingdom Hearts you can.

00:01:52.000 --> 00:02:01.780
But it's a top-down, overworld, RPG, story, ARPG, beat-em-up battles game where you play

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:05.540
as Jack and Ryu, two little ninjas, hence the name.

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:07.220
Get a little of those guys.

00:02:07.340 --> 00:02:08.400
They're brothers too.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:17.760
And they're wandering China-land to stop the Yoma clan and their leader, Blue Boltar, in

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:24.220
what is kind of like a rather large, sweeping, Final Fantasy-style RPG story.

00:02:24.780 --> 00:02:31.860
It has, as I mentioned, those ARPG fights, but there is like, you know, the idea of a

00:02:31.860 --> 00:02:37.980
quest and going to towns and collecting information to figure out where to go next.

00:02:37.980 --> 00:02:43.240
And there is even some light turn-based battling with the bosses.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:46.040
So this game has a lot to offer.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:54.260
Maybe most importantly, the headline here, you can play as Jack and Ryu with another player.

00:02:54.260 --> 00:03:00.220
You can do a cooperative experience here where at any moment in your file, you can just have

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:04.900
another person show up and be like, hey, you want to just play this RPG with me?

00:03:05.140 --> 00:03:06.300
That's kind of nuts.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:12.360
This complete, like, drop-in, drop-out co-op multiplayer, have we seen that before on the

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:14.060
NES? Like that, I didn't know that.

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:17.260
I guess I just, now that I think about it, I don't know why it wouldn't be possible,

00:03:17.260 --> 00:03:20.080
but it just seems like something that is illegal on the NES.

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.520
It's illegal.

00:03:22.220 --> 00:03:24.780
Yeah, I was very surprised.

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:30.100
And the convenience store, it really is convenient in that regard.

00:03:32.080 --> 00:03:33.800
I appreciate it a lot.

00:03:33.900 --> 00:03:36.000
I just didn't have anyone to share it with.

00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:38.400
Well, this is another one of those.

00:03:38.580 --> 00:03:39.460
Well, Joe, don't say same.

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:41.280
Did we play this together?

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:43.380
We did. This is one of those ones that we played.

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:48.300
I don't remember. That's why when I booted this up, I was like, we already played this game.

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:51.540
And then I was like, oh, this must be Kung Fu Heroes.

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.540
And I looked back at that, like, this doesn't look anything like that.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:56.380
You and I played it, but for like a minute.

00:03:57.240 --> 00:04:01.780
Yes, yeah. Well, what we did was, is because we just wanted to see how it worked, because obviously we're

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:06.820
not going to, like, start a sweeping RPG in the middle of the night.

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:09.880
But what was interesting was, is that the trade-off, of course.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:12.740
We did. You slept over the night before.

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:16.940
Yeah, but you guys didn't invite me to play Little Ninja Brothers, is all I'm saying.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:18.440
Yeah, we were like, it's only two player.

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:19.580
We can't let him know about it.

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:27.720
He'll get upset. And what we found out was that there is no, like, there is no separate Jack and Ryu.

00:04:27.900 --> 00:04:29.180
They are a package deal.

00:04:29.180 --> 00:04:34.740
And so, like, the second player just has whatever the first player has as well.

00:04:34.860 --> 00:04:39.460
You don't, like, have separate equipment for that second character.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.020
Right, but it does make a big...

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:42.260
So that's, like, the little trick that they're doing.

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.080
It does seemingly, I should say, make a big difference in these battles.

00:04:46.200 --> 00:04:49.160
Like, it doesn't look like it gives you, oh, you're playing two player, you're going

00:04:49.160 --> 00:04:51.340
to have double the enemies to fight or anything like that.

00:04:51.440 --> 00:04:52.280
It's just... No, yeah, it doesn't.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:55.400
It just halved the difficulty because you have a second person.

00:04:55.760 --> 00:05:00.860
Exactly. I think that the whole game is balanced for having two people play, and I'm sure we'll get

00:05:00.860 --> 00:05:02.000
to that later. Yeah.

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:09.600
But, you know, on the map and in towns, it feels like a Dragon Quest parody in a way.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.780
Like, it's not taking itself overly serious, but it follows the same kind of story beats

00:05:13.780 --> 00:05:15.820
as one of those fantasy RPGs.

00:05:17.420 --> 00:05:20.760
When a fight starts, obviously, it turns into that Kung Fu Heroes.

00:05:20.920 --> 00:05:24.460
Joe, I don't know if you actually remember that game, but we did record it many years

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:25.420
ago. Yeah.

00:05:25.420 --> 00:05:27.580
And that is the... I actually listened to that episode this week.

00:05:27.720 --> 00:05:29.780
Okay, great. That is the...

00:05:30.360 --> 00:05:31.200
Was it essential?

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.400
...from the same series. No, it was not.

00:05:33.980 --> 00:05:34.880
Sam was on it.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:36.660
Oh, shit.

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:43.800
But that series is called Super Chinese in Japan, and that's kind of confusing.

00:05:43.940 --> 00:05:45.540
Do they call it that in China as well?

00:05:46.120 --> 00:05:48.020
Super Chinese China Land World.

00:05:48.460 --> 00:05:49.560
Yeah, yeah, exactly.

00:05:50.180 --> 00:05:52.720
And so that was Super Chinese 1.

00:05:52.720 --> 00:05:56.920
This is Super Chinese 2, and it's like kind of a big difference because the Kung Fu Heroes

00:05:56.920 --> 00:05:58.900
game is like an arcade game.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:04.440
And now they're taking that element of that arcade game and making it how you do random

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:11.100
encounters instead of, you know, the traditional turn-based battles where you just kind of input

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:14.300
your commands. What do we think about that?

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:23.180
I'm calling it ARPG, but it is kind of more like beat-em-up or brawler gameplay element in

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:33.660
this RPG. I like the idea of moving from sort of like remixing this whole random encounter, like

00:06:33.660 --> 00:06:39.060
JRPG thing and actually having it be not just a menu that you're interacting with.

00:06:39.560 --> 00:06:41.940
I think that's a fun mix.

00:06:42.560 --> 00:06:43.820
Joe, what were you going to say?

00:06:44.620 --> 00:06:47.600
I was just going to say at first, I do really enjoy this.

00:06:47.700 --> 00:06:48.540
It was kind of the same thing.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:55.060
Like, I love a JRPG, I love turn-based combat, but it is really cool to see it switched up.

00:06:56.540 --> 00:07:01.720
But, you know, I can't pretend that I didn't get a little tired of getting into these battles

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:03.980
after, you know, a few hours of play.

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:07.020
But like, I mean, that's true for turn-based combat too.

00:07:07.220 --> 00:07:11.620
So, you know, I don't think it's a, I don't think, I think it's a, it's a nice refresher

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:13.540
for sure. Yeah, what's worse?

00:07:15.120 --> 00:07:23.240
Cycling through the same menus through really, like, you know, you don't really need to fight

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:24.620
these guys, but they just keep coming.

00:07:25.800 --> 00:07:31.140
Or having something a little bit more engaging, but a bit more of like a time waster.

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.860
Like, what is, what's the worst kind of grind?

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:40.260
I mean, I hate to say it for me personally, because I'm always kind of a big defender of

00:07:40.260 --> 00:07:43.460
turn-based combat among the friends of mine that aren't into that.

00:07:43.740 --> 00:07:50.540
But I think that that might be worse if you're like, a lot of turn-based games now, I really

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:56.780
do think need to like balance it in such a way where you're not just doing a bunch of

00:07:56.780 --> 00:08:01.400
menuing over and over again for, you know, for, for 20 hours of just grinding where there's

00:08:01.400 --> 00:08:06.000
no strategy involved, whereas this at least, yes, is a time waster, but at least there's

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.240
still that twitch reflex thing.

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:10.900
So it's still, every encounter still can feel a little different because enemies can move

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:12.340
differently or whatever, you're in a fight.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:18.240
It's close, but I do think that this does help mitigate that a little bit for like-

00:08:18.240 --> 00:08:22.980
I agree. A turn-based game where like you're, you're so deep in, you just got to grind and it's

00:08:22.980 --> 00:08:24.920
like, well, there's no fun anymore.

00:08:25.280 --> 00:08:30.860
You're taking a very active role when you're doing this kind of brawler-based combat where you

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:36.140
actually like can't just turn off your mind and spam A on, you know, the generic attack

00:08:36.140 --> 00:08:39.880
button because you know that you're stronger than the enemy and they might chip away a little

00:08:39.880 --> 00:08:42.800
bit of damage, but nothing a potion can't solve in between battles.

00:08:42.980 --> 00:08:44.880
That kind of repetitive grind.

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:51.420
But it is missing maybe more of the like in your head story flair that turn-based combat

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:56.700
sometimes gives of like how, you know, like the decisions you make and like the story you

00:08:56.700 --> 00:09:00.760
create in your head of that battle is more exciting than the battle that plays out here

00:09:00.760 --> 00:09:02.520
in this like, all right.

00:09:02.580 --> 00:09:07.160
And so I just like move up the screen and I punch this guy in the face and he's gone and

00:09:07.160 --> 00:09:10.320
then I punch this guy and there's still guys around, but for whatever reason, I think I've

00:09:10.320 --> 00:09:11.360
cleared enough guys.

00:09:11.400 --> 00:09:13.360
So the battle just decides to end.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:16.940
It's kind of weird that you don't have to like kill everybody in order for the battle

00:09:16.940 --> 00:09:18.280
to end. It's like, did they run away?

00:09:18.500 --> 00:09:20.560
What happened? Because they kind of get blown away.

00:09:21.120 --> 00:09:22.640
Yeah. I always thought they ran away.

00:09:23.380 --> 00:09:25.760
I like Joe's interpretation here.

00:09:26.900 --> 00:09:34.480
I've never, I think in like in a generic random encounter, I don't think I've ever had a story

00:09:34.480 --> 00:09:41.940
in my head about how a turn-based battle went, but I would find your description of it of

00:09:41.940 --> 00:09:45.940
like, oh, you killed enough of them and that just means it's over.

00:09:45.940 --> 00:09:50.940
However, that, that is weird, but Joe's description of they run away.

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:53.340
Uh, I, I, I like that one better.

00:09:53.640 --> 00:09:59.840
I also find it kind of interesting the way that you, um, have to only, only kill a certain

00:09:59.840 --> 00:10:04.720
amount because again, gameplay wise, it can make you like decide like, well, who do I want

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:08.680
to fight here? Sometimes that you have multiple forms of enemies on the screen at once and like one's

00:10:08.680 --> 00:10:09.940
a real pain in the ass and the other's not.

00:10:10.080 --> 00:10:13.820
You can hope that there's enough of the other one to just kill all of them and avoid the

00:10:13.820 --> 00:10:15.700
one you don't want to fight and then they'll run away.

00:10:15.940 --> 00:10:18.520
Does that affect the amount of experience you get?

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:20.420
I don't know. Yeah.

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:27.440
I don't, I don't know either, but I always avoided the weird, like crunchy balls with teeth

00:10:27.440 --> 00:10:29.120
and whatever else.

00:10:29.380 --> 00:10:31.580
Like just, I hate those little totodile heads.

00:10:31.960 --> 00:10:39.920
Yeah. The way the game starts, uh, you have an emergency TV broadcast, uh, that cuts in and the, uh,

00:10:40.060 --> 00:10:44.900
the blue bolt are the leader of the, uh, Yoma clan announces that he's kidnapped the

00:10:44.900 --> 00:10:46.700
emperor and taken over China land.

00:10:47.160 --> 00:10:50.560
Uh, you know, that's kind of, that's pretty funny.

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:57.620
Uh, does seeing the world ending threat via TV broadcast make this feel more modern than

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:03.840
like the other RPGs we get where, you know, the King summons you to the throne room or you

00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:08.360
have to like start out on some quest that isn't really part of the main quest.

00:11:08.560 --> 00:11:13.800
And then it turns out like everything was, was for not like, is this just like a more,

00:11:14.500 --> 00:11:19.640
uh, straightforward way of getting the player's attention and modernizing RPGs a little?

00:11:19.640 --> 00:11:24.580
Yeah. That's, that's a good, that's a good, uh, way to phrase it.

00:11:24.780 --> 00:11:27.120
I, I appreciated it.

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:34.820
Um, I think when you, when you said earlier that this is just a big parody of JRPGs, I,

00:11:34.820 --> 00:11:36.100
I totally agree with you.

00:11:36.100 --> 00:11:42.280
Um, I didn't, I haven't played the vast majority of this game to be honest, but of what I did

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:50.380
play, it does seem like it's just playing around with the storytelling tropes that you

00:11:50.380 --> 00:11:53.620
see usually. And it's all just kind of nonsense.

00:11:53.620 --> 00:12:00.720
And I think learning about the big bad via a TV broadcast is right in line with that.

00:12:00.920 --> 00:12:01.860
So I'm a fan.

00:12:02.220 --> 00:12:08.220
Yeah. Cause the tone of everything is kind of like just played for not, not like it's, it's the

00:12:08.220 --> 00:12:12.920
funniest thing ever, but it's played for like gentle comedy, you know, towns have fun names,

00:12:13.080 --> 00:12:14.460
NPCs crack jokes.

00:12:14.980 --> 00:12:20.400
Uh, it's, it's a save the world story, but it's kind of wrapped in this goofy kid-friendly

00:12:20.400 --> 00:12:25.980
packaging. Gentle, kid-friendly, uh, cannibalism and stuff like that.

00:12:26.180 --> 00:12:27.320
Yeah. Sure.

00:12:27.740 --> 00:12:29.040
Yeah. There, there's, there's moments.

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:31.340
Yes. But like think about the town names, right?

00:12:31.440 --> 00:12:35.000
You have, um, Deli Chews, which is delicious.

00:12:35.740 --> 00:12:39.300
Uh, Silly City, but it's with a C instead of an S.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:40.560
Silly City is fun.

00:12:41.200 --> 00:12:47.160
Right. Because not only is it, not only is that silly, but it's a, it's a city where the people

00:12:47.160 --> 00:12:50.300
in the town have turned, like they're, they've become idiots.

00:12:50.300 --> 00:12:51.420
Stupid. Uh, yeah.

00:12:51.740 --> 00:12:56.500
Yeah. And someone in, I think someone in Delish-yus or whatever you call that town says like,

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:59.940
yeah, everyone over there turned stupid because of like a spell or something.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:03.660
It's just funny. Like, oh, a spell went over and turned over to all the citizens stupid.

